faq:
  - q: "What do recruiters evaluate on a phone screen?"
    a: "Five things: (1) Communication clarity — can you explain what you do in 90 seconds? (2) Role fit signal — does your experience match the level and specialization they are hiring for? (3) Motivation — do you have a clear reason for this specific company and role? (4) Compensation alignment — is your range compatible with what the role pays? (5) Green flags and red flags — enthusiasm, preparation, and specific knowledge of the company signals; vagueness, negativity about current employer, and unclear next-role criteria signals."
  - q: "How do I answer 'what are you looking for?' on a phone screen?"
    a: "Be specific. 'I am looking for a Staff engineer role at a Series B–D company working on infrastructure or platform at scale. Ideally with strong engineering culture and room to influence technical direction.' Vague answers ('I'm open to anything') signal poor self-knowledge and low motivation."
  - q: "How should I answer salary questions on a phone screen?"
    a: "Do not give a number first if you can avoid it. Redirect: 'I am open to a competitive package — what is the range budgeted for this role?' If pushed: give a range based on your research, with the bottom of your range at your target. Do not anchor too low."
  - q: "How long is a recruiter phone screen?"
    a: "20–30 minutes typically. They will introduce the role, ask 3–5 screening questions, answer your questions, and confirm next steps. Prepare 3 specific questions about the role, team, or product — general questions signal low preparation."
